---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Q1 = `let steps = 0;
let n = 25;

for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
  for (let j = 0; j < 4; j++) {
    steps = steps + 1;
  }
}

console.log(steps);`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Question Set 12</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">Six questions &middot; 11 minutes &middot; cumulative</h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            Six questions through Activity 2.14, weighted toward{" "}
            <b>algorithms, efficiency, and what cannot be computed</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Sessions 36 to 40 are the highest-value ground on the whole
            exam.</b> Algorithm reasoning is the largest single slice of the
            multiple-choice section. This set is worth more attention than its
            two points suggest.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Set a timer for 11 minutes.</b> Work them out on paper; do not
            run the code. Mark each answer <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>. Guess
            rather than leave a blank.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="questions">
          <h2>The Questions</h2>

          <p>
            Four have exactly one correct answer, one asks you to select
            <i>all</i> that apply, and the last is about data or the impact of
            computing rather than about code.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 1</h3>

          <p>What does this display?</p>

          <Code code={Q1} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Question 1" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />

          <ul>
            <li>(A) 25</li>
            <li>(B) 100</li>
            <li>(C) 625</li>
            <li>(D) 4</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 2</h3>

          <p>
            The number of steps an algorithm takes grows like <b>n cubed</b> as
            the input grows. This algorithm is described as running in:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Unreasonable time</li>
            <li>(B) Constant time</li>
            <li>(C) No time at all</li>
            <li>(D) Reasonable time</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 3 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>Which of these are true of a <b>heuristic</b>?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) It guarantees the best possible answer</li>
            <li>(B) It produces an answer in a reasonable amount of time</li>
            <li>(C) It is used when finding the exact best answer would take too long</li>
            <li>(D) Its answer may be worse than the best possible one</li>
            <li>(E) It makes an undecidable problem decidable</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 4</h3>

          <p>
            A problem for which no algorithm can be written that always gives a
            correct yes-or-no answer is called:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Undecidable</li>
            <li>(B) Unreasonable</li>
            <li>(C) Unsorted</li>
            <li>(D) A heuristic</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 5</h3>

          <p>
            Selection sort runs on a list of 6 numbers that is{" "}
            <b>already in order</b>. How many comparisons does it make?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) 0</li>
            <li>(B) 5</li>
            <li>(C) 15</li>
            <li>(D) 6</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 6 &mdash; Data and Impact</h3>

          <p>
            A company builds a tool to rank job applications. It is trained on
            the company's own hiring records from the past ten years, during
            which the company rarely hired women as engineers. The tool now ranks
            applications from women lower. This is best described as:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) A heuristic</li>
            <li>(B) Bias in a computing innovation</li>
            <li>(C) Data compression</li>
            <li>(D) Redundancy</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Six answers, each marked <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>, plus how far
            you got when the timer went off. Worth <b>2 points</b>, graded on
            completion.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Start the Timer
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
